Yes! You can display the output of a C program on an HTML webpage without Google App Engine (GAE).

Use HTML + C (CGI) + Apache Server.

### 1. C program (`odd.c`)

```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main()
{
    int n;

    printf("Content-Type: text/html\n\n");

    printf("<html><body>");
    printf("<h2>Nth Odd Number</h2>");

    n = 5;
    printf("Nth Odd Number = %d", 2*n-1);

    printf("</body></html>");

    return 0;
}
```

### 2. Compile the C program

```
gcc odd.c -o /usr/lib/cgi-bin/odd.cgi
```

### 3. Enable CGI in Apache

```
sudo a2enmod cgi
sudo systemctl restart apache2
```

### 4. Run in browser

Open:

```
http://localhost/cgi-bin/odd.cgi
```

Expected browser output

## Nth Odd Number

Nth Odd Number = 9

Note: This example uses a fixed input of 5. You can extend it with an HTML form to accept N from the user and pass it to the C program.

No GAE required.




For running your C program on an HTML webpage using Apache CGI (without GAE) in Ubuntu, execute these commands.

### 1. Install required packages

```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install apache2 gcc -y
```

### 2. Enable CGI module

```
sudo a2enmod cgi
```

### 3. Start Apache server

```
sudo systemctl start apache2
sudo systemctl enable apache2
sudo systemctl restart apache2
```

### 4. Create CGI directory (if required)

```
sudo mkdir -p /usr/lib/cgi-bin
```

### 5. Check Apache

Open your browser:

```
http://localhost
```

You should see the Apache2 Ubuntu Default Page.

### 6. Create and compile C program

```
nano odd.c
```

After writing your C code, compile:

```
gcc odd.c -o odd.cgi
```

Move it into CGI directory:

```
sudo mv odd.cgi /usr/lib/cgi-bin/
sudo chmod 755 /usr/lib/cgi-bin/odd.cgi
```

### 7. Run in browser

```
http://localhost/cgi-bin/odd.cgi
```

Required software installed

| Software | Purpose                           |
| -------- | --------------------------------- |
| Apache2  | Web server                        |
| GCC      | C compiler                        |
| CGI      | Execute C program through browser |

Important: Your C program must print the HTTP header `Content-Type: text/html` before printing HTML content.
